  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is given by ${f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u,\\,u=3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is \n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=0.\n$$",
